  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "If we follow the graph from left to right, we can see that: \n\n- For $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)$ the graph falls\n\n- For $x\\in (-3,-2)$ the graph rises\n\n- For $x\\in (-2,-1)$ the graph falls\n\n- For $x\\in (-1,0)$ the graph rises\n\n- For $x\\in (0,1)$ the graph falls\n\n- For $x\\in (1,2)$ the graph rises\n\n- For $x\\in (2,3)$ the graph falls\n\n- For $x\\in (3,\\infty )$ the graph rises\n\nThe function is increasing on the intervals where it never falls: $(-3,-2),$  $(-1,0),$  $(1,2),$ and $(3,\\infty ).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
